WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Bacteria don't make human insulin, and cotton plants

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.760
don't make a caterpillar poison.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.200
Yet since the nineteen-eighties, bacteria have made human

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.480
insulin for people with diabetes, and most Australian

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.520
cotton now makes its own insect toxin.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.640
Both carry DNA from another species.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.120
So how do scientists cut out a useful

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.080
piece of DNA, join it into a carrier,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.160
get it into a host, and make sure

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.920
the host actually uses it?

00:00:29.134 --> 00:00:31.773
First, you need the right piece of DNA.

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:36.173
Remember that most human genes contain introns, stretches

00:00:36.254 --> 00:00:38.573
our cells cut out of the mRNA before

00:00:38.654 --> 00:00:39.213
it's read.

00:00:39.774 --> 00:00:42.653
Bacteria can't do that cutting, so a gene

00:00:42.854 --> 00:00:45.213
taken straight from a human chromosome would give

00:00:45.294 --> 00:00:46.653
them a scrambled message.

00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:50.893
Instead, scientists use a version with no introns,

00:00:51.014 --> 00:00:53.853
copied from the cell's finished mRNA, or built

00:00:53.934 --> 00:00:54.653
from scratch.

00:00:55.294 --> 00:00:57.613
For the first bacterial insulin, in the late

00:00:57.694 --> 00:01:01.933
nineteen-seventies, scientists built DNA for insulin's two chains

00:01:02.014 --> 00:01:02.973
from scratch.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.680
Next, cut.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.360
A restriction enzyme is an enzyme that cuts

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.640
DNA only at one particular short sequence.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.520
Bacteria make these enzymes to chop up the

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.840
DNA of invading viruses.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.920
Many of them cut the two strands at

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.520
slightly different points, leaving a few unpaired bases

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.200
hanging off each end.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.680
These are called sticky ends, because they can

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.840
pair with matching bases.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.720
Scientists open the plasmid with the same enzyme,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.040
or one that leaves matching ends, so the

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.520
plasmid's ends fit the gene's ends.

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:39.213
Then, paste.

00:01:39.494 --> 00:01:41.813
Mix the gene with the opened plasmids, and

00:01:41.894 --> 00:01:44.773
the sticky ends pair up, base to base.

00:01:44.814 --> 00:01:47.093
But they're only held by weak hydrogen bonds

00:01:47.174 --> 00:01:48.773
between the bases.

00:01:48.854 --> 00:01:51.493
DNA ligase is the enzyme that seals the

00:01:51.614 --> 00:01:54.133
backbone of each strand, joining the pieces for

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:55.173
good.

00:01:55.294 --> 00:01:58.453
Now the plasmid holds DNA from two sources:

00:01:58.534 --> 00:02:00.613
that's recombinant DNA.

00:02:00.694 --> 00:02:03.093
The plasmid also carries a gene for resistance

00:02:03.174 --> 00:02:05.893
to an antibiotic, a marker that will matter

00:02:05.974 --> 00:02:06.693
in a moment.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.100
Now, put it in.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.220
Bacteria don't take up plasmids easily.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.660
So scientists chill them in calcium chloride, which

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.540
helps DNA stick to the cell surface, add

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.260
the plasmids, then give a quick heat shock,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.660
which briefly lets DNA into the cell.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.340
Taking up DNA like this is called transformation.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.220
Even then, only a small fraction of the

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.980
bacteria take in a plasmid.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.440
So, pick.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.240
Spread the bacteria on agar containing the antibiotic.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.520
Only bacteria carrying a plasmid have the resistance

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.800
gene, so only they grow into colonies.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.867
But surviving doesn't prove the gene is there,

00:02:47.973 --> 00:02:50.400
because some plasmids close up without it.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.800
So a second check, like a second marker

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.920
gene or reading the DNA, finds the colonies

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.680
with the gene inside.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.480
Each of those colonies is a clone, copying

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.560
the gene every time it divides.

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:06.166
Copying the gene isn't the same as using

00:03:06.247 --> 00:03:06.686
it.

00:03:06.967 --> 00:03:09.126
To make the protein, the gene must sit

00:03:09.207 --> 00:03:12.006
next to a promoter that the bacterium's RNA

00:03:12.127 --> 00:03:13.846
polymerase recognises.

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:16.326
Then the bacterium reads it like one of

00:03:16.447 --> 00:03:18.886
its own genes, because the genetic code is

00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:21.926
shared: the same codons mean the same amino

00:03:22.007 --> 00:03:24.566
acids in bacteria and in us.

00:03:25.047 --> 00:03:27.686
The first insulin bacteria made the two chains

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:30.966
separately, and chemists purified them and joined them

00:03:31.087 --> 00:03:32.326
into insulin.

00:03:32.527 --> 00:03:35.926
Today, huge tanks of engineered microbes make it.

00:03:37.134 --> 00:03:39.693
Plants need a different carrier, because the gene

00:03:39.774 --> 00:03:41.933
has to cross a tough cell wall and

00:03:42.014 --> 00:03:44.093
end up in the plant's own chromosomes.

00:03:44.734 --> 00:03:48.893
A soil bacterium called Agrobacterium does this naturally:

00:03:48.974 --> 00:03:51.453
it inserts a piece of its plasmid DNA

00:03:51.534 --> 00:03:53.373
into a plant cell's chromosomes.

00:03:54.094 --> 00:03:57.133
Scientists remove its disease-causing genes and put the

00:03:57.294 --> 00:03:58.733
useful gene in instead.

00:03:59.294 --> 00:04:01.613
That's how Bt cotton was first made.

00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:04.573
A gene for a caterpillar toxin, from another

00:04:04.654 --> 00:04:07.693
soil bacterium, went into cotton cells, which were

00:04:07.894 --> 00:04:09.293
grown into whole plants.

00:04:09.934 --> 00:04:12.493
Every cell of those plants carries the gene,

00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:14.253
and it's passed on in their seeds.

00:04:14.894 --> 00:04:17.453
An organism given DNA from another species this

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:19.213
way is transgenic.

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:23.793
Here's the whole method in four words: cut,

00:04:23.874 --> 00:04:26.153
paste, put in, pick.

00:04:26.514 --> 00:04:29.873
Cut with a restriction enzyme, paste with ligase,

00:04:29.954 --> 00:04:32.273
put the plasmid into the host, and pick

00:04:32.354 --> 00:04:34.233
the hosts that carry the gene.

00:04:34.514 --> 00:04:37.233
And remember why the cut matters: the same

00:04:37.314 --> 00:04:39.713
enzyme gives matching sticky ends.

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:42.453
Two exam questions now.

00:04:43.014 --> 00:04:43.493
First.

00:04:44.214 --> 00:04:47.093
Scientists have an intron-free copy of the calf

00:04:47.174 --> 00:04:49.813
gene for chymosin, an enzyme used to make

00:04:49.894 --> 00:04:52.773
cheese, and a plasmid carrying a gene for

00:04:52.854 --> 00:04:55.253
resistance to the antibiotic ampicillin.

00:04:55.974 --> 00:04:57.613
Describe how they could produce E.

00:04:57.654 --> 00:05:00.933
coli bacteria that carry the chymosin gene, and

00:05:01.014 --> 00:05:03.013
how they could identify those bacteria.

00:05:03.654 --> 00:05:05.093
Pause here to answer.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.420
Cut the gene and the plasmid with the

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.540
same restriction enzyme, so their sticky ends match.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.740
Mix them, and ligase seals the joins.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.580
Heat-shock the bacteria so some take up a

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.020
plasmid, then grow them on ampicillin.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.620
Only bacteria with a plasmid survive, so check

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.620
those colonies for the chymosin gene.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:37.980
Saying every colony that survives must carry the

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.220
gene is the classic mistake.

00:05:41.434 --> 00:05:41.913
Second.

00:05:41.994 --> 00:05:45.353
A student puts a human gene, copied directly

00:05:45.434 --> 00:05:48.073
from a chromosome, into a plasmid beside a

00:05:48.154 --> 00:05:49.353
bacterial promoter.

00:05:49.914 --> 00:05:52.153
The bacteria make a faulty protein.

00:05:52.714 --> 00:05:54.713
A copy of the same gene made from

00:05:54.794 --> 00:05:57.753
its mRNA, placed the same way, gives the

00:05:57.834 --> 00:05:58.793
working protein.

00:05:59.354 --> 00:06:00.913
Explain these results.

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:02.553
Pause here to answer.

00:06:12.967 --> 00:06:15.206
The copy from the chromosome still has its

00:06:15.287 --> 00:06:16.006
introns.

00:06:16.407 --> 00:06:19.526
Bacteria can't cut introns out, so they read

00:06:19.607 --> 00:06:21.766
them as code, and the protein comes out

00:06:21.847 --> 00:06:22.246
wrong.

00:06:22.807 --> 00:06:25.766
The copy made from mRNA has its introns

00:06:25.847 --> 00:06:29.766
already removed, so nothing interrupts the coding sequence,

00:06:29.807 --> 00:06:31.926
and the bacteria read it using the same

00:06:32.007 --> 00:06:33.526
genetic code we do.

00:06:33.847 --> 00:06:36.646
Saying bacteria use a different genetic code is

00:06:36.687 --> 00:06:38.166
the classic mistake.

00:06:39.367 --> 00:06:43.206
So to describe recombinant DNA technology, name each

00:06:43.287 --> 00:06:45.286
tool and its job, in order.

00:06:45.927 --> 00:06:50.166
Get intron-free DNA, cut with a restriction enzyme,

00:06:50.247 --> 00:06:53.526
join with ligase, put it into a host,

00:06:53.647 --> 00:06:55.846
pick the hosts that carry the gene, and

00:06:55.927 --> 00:06:57.686
make sure the host can read it.

00:06:57.927 --> 00:07:00.086
Then link the method to its product.

00:07:00.647 --> 00:07:04.006
Next, which benefits of these technologies really hold

00:07:04.087 --> 00:07:04.486
up.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.140
Here are the key notes.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.300
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:10.180
to copy them.
